exactly this statement. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.thm-critical-dyadic-band"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.thm-critical-dyadic-band"],"id":"long.thm-critical-dyadic-band","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.43 (Reduction to the nearest dyadic boundary)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.43, p. 56, label thm:critical-dyadic-band","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Let $d,E$ be nonnegative integers with $E\\le2^{d+1}$, and let $j_*$ be the largest $j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}$ for which $E\\le2^{d-j+1}$. The set is nonempty, so $j_*$ is well defined, including when $E=0$. Then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n &\\forall j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,d\\},\\quad\n  2^{d-j+1}<E\\ \\text{or}\\ E+2(d+j)\\le2^{d-j+1}\\\\\n &\\hspace{35mm}\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n E+2(d+j_*)\\le2^{d-j_*+1}.\n \\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor $j>j_*$ the first alternative holds by maximality. For $j\\le j_*$, the left side of the required inequality increases with $j$ and its right side decreases, so the condition at $j_*$ implies all the others. This is an elementary reduction from $d+1$ inequalities to one. Specialized to the seam reset charge (where the actual upper-reset condition gives $E\\le2^{d+1}$, as explained in Proposition 6.80), the reduced hypothesis at the single index $j_*$ is proved logically equivalent to Theorem 6.10’s band-avoidance hypothesis.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-critical-dyadic-band","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-critical-dyadic-band","id":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-critical-dyadic-band%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.HalfUpperResetCriticalBand.dyadicBandEscape_iff_exists_critical%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:f31ae